<div class="landing landing-home">
  <div class="home-top">
    <.marketing_nav current_user={assigns[:current_user]} />

    <section class="home-hero">
      <div class="home-wrap">
        <p class="home-eyebrow">The layer between a website and its owner</p>
        <h1>The website defines its own CMS.</h1>
        <div class="home-hero-row">
          <p class="home-lede">
            On Masthead, whoever builds a website also decides how it gets managed.
            The theme declares what its owner can change. Masthead turns that into
            the site's admin, then hosts, publishes and serves the site.
          </p>
          <div class="home-actions">
            <%= if assigns[:current_user] do %>
              <.link navigate={~p"/sites"} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">
                Open dashboard &rarr;
              </.link>
            <% else %>
              <.link navigate={~p"/signup"} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">
                Create a free site
              </.link>
              <a
                href="https://docs.masthead.site/posts/creating-a-custom-theme"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener"
                class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg"
              >
                Build a theme
              </a>
            <% end %>
          </div>
        </div>

        <ol class="home-flow" aria-label="How Masthead sits between a website and its owner">
          <li>
            <strong>Theme</strong>
            <span>Declares what its owner can change, in its manifest.</span>
            <i class="home-flow-line" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </li>
          <li class="home-flow-accent">
            <strong>Masthead</strong>
            <span>Builds the admin from that declaration, and runs the site.</span>
            <i class="home-flow-line" aria-hidden="true"></i>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Owner</strong>
            <span>Manages the content and the settings that matter. Nothing else.</span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <section class="home-band" aria-labelledby="inversion-title">
    <div class="home-wrap">
      <h2 id="inversion-title">Most CMSs come first.<br />On Masthead, the website does.</h2>

      <div class="home-compare">
        <div class="home-compare-col">
          <h3>A typical CMS</h3>
          <ol>
            <li class="home-compare-node">A generic CMS</li>
            <li class="home-compare-step">ships the same admin to every site</li>
            <li class="home-compare-node">A generic editor</li>
            <li class="home-compare-step">the website is fitted inside it</li>
            <li class="home-compare-node">The website</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="home-compare-col home-compare-new">
          <h3>Masthead</h3>
          <ol>
            <li class="home-compare-node">The website and its theme</li>
            <li class="home-compare-step">declares what needs managing</li>
            <li class="home-compare-node home-compare-accent">Masthead</li>
            <li class="home-compare-step">builds the admin for that site</li>
            <li class="home-compare-node">A management experience made for this website</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="home-note">
        A typical CMS is a finished product, and every website has to fit inside it.
        Masthead reverses the order. The website comes first and tells Masthead what
        its owner should be able to manage. The admin follows from the website.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band home-band-ink" aria-labelledby="theme-title">
    <div class="home-wrap">
      <div class="home-head">
        <h2 id="theme-title">A theme is a website and the admin that goes with it.</h2>
        <p>
          A Masthead theme has Liquid templates, a stylesheet and a <code>manifest.json</code>.
          The manifest is where the website says what can be managed. This is the theme
          every site starts with, next to the admin Masthead builds from it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="home-declare">
        <figure class="home-code">
          <figcaption>manifest.json · Default theme (abridged)</figcaption>
          <pre phx-no-curly-interpolation><code>"tokens": [
  { "key": "accent", "type": "color",
    "label": "Accent color", "category": "Appearance" },
  { "key": "favicon", "type": "file",
    "label": "Favicon", "category": "Appearance" },
  { "key": "show_search", "type": "boolean",
    "label": "Show post search", "category": "Posts" }
],
"post_options": [
  { "key": "featured_image", "type": "file",
    "label": "Featured image" },
  { "key": "seo_description", "type": "text",
    "label": "Search description" }
],
"page_options": [
  { "key": "layout", "type": "select",
    "options": ["contained", "wide"] }
]</code></pre>
        </figure>

        <div class="home-admin" aria-label="The admin Masthead builds from this manifest">
          <div class="home-admin-bar">Admin · generated from the manifest</div>
          <div class="home-admin-section">
            <span class="home-admin-title">Theme customization</span>
            <span class="home-admin-group">Appearance</span>
            <div class="home-admin-field">
              <span>Accent color</span><span class="home-admin-swatch"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="home-admin-field">
              <span>Favicon</span><span class="home-admin-input">Choose file</span>
            </div>
            <span class="home-admin-group">Posts</span>
            <div class="home-admin-field">
              <span>Show post search</span><span class="home-admin-toggle"></span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="home-admin-section">
            <span class="home-admin-title">New post · Post options</span>
            <div class="home-admin-field">
              <span>Featured image</span><span class="home-admin-input">Choose file</span>
            </div>
            <div class="home-admin-field home-admin-field-text">
              <span>Search description</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="home-admin-section">
            <span class="home-admin-title">New page · Page settings</span>
            <div class="home-admin-field">
              <span>Page layout</span><span class="home-admin-input home-admin-select">contained</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul class="home-columns">
        <li>
          <h3>Settings</h3>
          <p>
            <code>tokens</code>
            declare site-wide settings like colours, sizes, images, switches and text.
            They come with labels, defaults and descriptions written by whoever built the theme.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Content structure</h3>
          <p>
            <code>post_options</code>
            and <code>page_options</code>
            add fields to every post and page. If the theme declares none, that step of the
            editor doesn't exist.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Page types</h3>
          <p>
            Page templates such as a gallery, a front page or a changelog each bring their own
            fields. The owner fills them in, and the template handles the layout.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="home-note">
        In most CMSs, a new theme changes how the site looks while the admin around it stays
        the same. On Masthead the admin changes with the theme, because it is built from the theme.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-split" aria-labelledby="sides-title">
    <h2 id="sides-title" class="home-split-title">
      <span class="home-wrap">
        The builder gets the complexity. The owner gets the simplicity.
      </span>
    </h2>
    <div class="home-split-halves">
      <div class="home-split-half home-split-builder">
        <div class="home-split-inner">
          <span class="home-split-role">The builder</span>
          <h3>Controls how it works</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Design</strong> in CSS and Liquid templates</li>
            <li><strong>Structure</strong>: which page types exist and what goes in them</li>
            <li><strong>Settings</strong>: what can change, with defaults and explanations</li>
            <li>
              <strong>The editing experience</strong>: the fields each post and page carries
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Behaviour</strong>: search, tags and navigation, if the theme exposes them
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="home-split-half home-split-owner">
        <div class="home-split-inner">
          <span class="home-split-role">The owner</span>
          <h3>Gets what it takes to run it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Content</strong>: posts and pages in Markdown or HTML</li>
            <li>
              <strong>Relevant settings</strong>, with the builder's explanations next to them
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Media</strong>: uploads, picked straight into the fields that use them
            </li>
            <li><strong>Their domain</strong>, with HTTPS taken care of</li>
            <li><strong>Their finished website</strong>, live when they publish</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Owners can handle more. They just shouldn't have to understand how their website
            was built in order to manage it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band" aria-labelledby="platform-title">
    <div class="home-wrap">
      <div class="home-head">
        <h2 id="platform-title">Everything around the definition.</h2>
        <p>
          A theme describes a website. Running that website takes more than templates.
          Masthead provides the rest, so a theme never has to.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ul class="home-platform">
        <li :for={
          {title, body, licensed?} <- [
            {"Hosting",
             "Every site is live on its own subdomain from the moment it is created, served over HTTPS.",
             false},
            {"Custom domains",
             "Point a domain at Masthead. It verifies ownership and provisions the TLS certificate.",
             true},
            {"Content storage",
             "Posts, pages, tags and drafts are stored once and rendered by whichever theme is active.",
             false},
            {"Media",
             "Uploads get thumbnails, can be compressed in the browser, and drop straight into theme fields.",
             false},
            {"Publishing",
             "Drafts, publish dates, search, tag-filtered pages and a homepage of your choosing.",
             false},
            {"Many sites, one account",
             "Each site has its own theme, content and domain, all from one dashboard.", false},
            {"Collaboration",
             "Invite people to a site and see who else is working in the admin, in real time.",
             true},
            {"Statistics",
             "Views and visitors per page, counted on the server with no cookies and no tracking script.",
             true},
            {"Discoverability",
             "sitemap.xml, robots.txt, llms.txt and structured data are generated for every site.",
             false},
            {"Stable rendering",
             "Renderers are versioned, so a site keeps rendering the way it did when it was built.",
             false},
            {"Imports",
             "Bring in a Hugo site, or single Markdown and HTML files with their frontmatter.",
             false},
            {"Site license",
             "Marked items come with a paid site license. Everything else is free on every site.",
             false}
          ]
        }>
          <h3>{title}<sup :if={licensed?} title="Requires a site license">*</sup></h3>
          <p>{body}</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band home-band-paper" aria-labelledby="proof-title">
    <div class="home-wrap">
      <h2 id="proof-title">Same platform.<br />Different websites. Different admins.</h2>

      <div class="home-pair">
        <a class="home-pair-site" href="https://docs.masthead.site" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          <img
            src={~p"/images/home/docs-site.webp"}
            alt="docs.masthead.site, a documentation site with a searchable sidebar"
            width="1280"
            height="760"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </a>
        <div class="home-pair-admin">
          <p class="home-pair-label">docs.masthead.site</p>
          <p>
            Our documentation runs on Masthead with the Documentation theme. The theme
            declares 35 settings in six groups, plus one page type. A few of them:
          </p>
          <dl>
            <div>
              <dt>Brand</dt>
              <dd>Logo, Sidebar title, Version badge</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Sidebar</dt>
              <dd>Show search box, Post list, Custom list</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Content</dt>
              <dd>Show "on this page", Show previous / next links</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Page types</dt>
              <dd>Documentation index</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>

      <ul class="home-gallery">
        <li :for={
          {name, kind, image, url, summary, sample} <- [
            {"Studio", "Long-form writing", "studio.webp",
             "https://masthead.site/marketplace/themes/111", "5 settings, no page types",
             "Accent color, Content width"},
            {"Halide", "Photography portfolio", "halide.webp",
             "https://masthead.site/marketplace/themes/115", "7 settings, 3 page types",
             "A Gallery page with a list of photos, each with caption and alt text"},
            {"Broadsheet", "Newspaper", "broadsheet.webp",
             "https://masthead.site/marketplace/themes/119", "30 settings, 4 page types",
             "Edition line, Drop cap on articles, Front page"},
            {"Launch", "Software launch site", "launch.webp",
             "https://masthead.site/marketplace/themes/120", "43 settings, 8 page types",
             "Announcement bar, Changelog, Feature deep-dive"}
          ]
        }>
          <a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            <img
              src={~p"/images/home/#{image}"}
              alt={"#{name} theme preview"}
              width="1200"
              height="680"
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <span class="home-gallery-name">{name} <em>{kind}</em></span>
            <span class="home-gallery-summary">{summary}</span>
            <span class="home-gallery-sample">{sample}</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="home-note">
        Five settings or forty-three: the theme decides.
        <.link navigate={~p"/marketplace"}>Browse the marketplace &rarr;</.link>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band" aria-labelledby="audience-title">
    <div class="home-wrap">
      <h2 id="audience-title">Build it once. Hand it over.</h2>

      <ol class="home-columns home-audience">
        <li>
          <h3>Agencies and independent developers</h3>
          <p>
            Build the website, decide exactly what your client can change, connect their
            domain and invite them in. The templates stay with you. The client gets an
            admin that contains their website and nothing else.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Theme developers</h3>
          <p>
            Build a theme once and define its structure and editing experience in its manifest.
            Keep it for your own sites or publish it to the marketplace. Ship updates by
            uploading a newer version.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Site owners</h3>
          <p>
            Get a website whose admin already fits it. Write, upload and adjust what the theme
            exposes. There are no plugins to update and you never have to look at a template.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band" aria-labelledby="not-title">
    <div class="home-wrap">
      <h2 id="not-title">What Masthead deliberately doesn't do.</h2>

      <ul class="home-nots">
        <li>
          <h3>It isn't a page builder.</h3>
          <p>
            Owners fill in the fields a theme defines. They don't drag blocks around,
            because layout belongs to the theme.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>It isn't an everything-CMS.</h3>
          <p>
            There is no plugin directory and no admin that grows with every install.
            Each site has one admin, shaped by its theme.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>It isn't an application framework.</h3>
          <p>
            Themes render content in a sandbox. They can't query the database, run server
            code or touch the filesystem.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>It isn't WordPress with a different interface.</h3>
          <p>
            New capability doesn't arrive as another checkbox. A website gets the controls
            its theme asks for.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <blockquote class="home-quote">
        We don't give site owners a thousand controls. The theme decides which ones they need.
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band home-band-ink" aria-labelledby="oss-title">
    <div class="home-wrap home-oss">
      <div>
        <h2 id="oss-title">Run it yourself, or read how it works.</h2>
        <p>
          Masthead is open source. You can inspect the code, build themes against it, change
          the platform, or run it on your own infrastructure: a Phoenix application with
          PostgreSQL, uploads on S3-compatible storage or local disk, and a Dockerfile included.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="home-oss-links">
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/dijkstrasoftware/masthead" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Source on GitHub
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a
            href="https://github.com/dijkstrasoftware/masthead-template"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            Theme starter template
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://docs.masthead.site/posts/self-hosting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Self-hosting docs
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-band" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
    <div class="home-wrap home-faq">
      <h2 id="faq-title">Questions</h2>
      <div>
        <details :for={{question, answer} <- @faqs}>
          <summary>{question}</summary>
          <p>{answer}</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="home-cta">
    <div class="home-wrap">
      <h2>See it from both sides.</h2>
      <p>Create a free site and pick a theme. Or write a theme and see the admin it produces.</p>
      <div class="home-actions">
        <%= if assigns[:current_user] do %>
          <.link navigate={~p"/sites"} class="btn btn-lg home-cta-primary">Open dashboard</.link>
        <% else %>
          <.link navigate={~p"/signup"} class="btn btn-lg home-cta-primary">
            Create a free site
          </.link>
          <a
            href="https://docs.masthead.site/posts/creating-a-custom-theme"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
            class="btn btn-lg home-cta-secondary"
          >
            Read the theme docs
          </a>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer current_user={assigns[:current_user]} />
</div>
